::GEO_U03_L03_Q01::
Question ID: GEO_U03_L03_Q01
MLA Standard: Readiness Support Only
Question:
If triangle ABC ≅ triangle DEF, which vertex corresponds to B?
{
=A. E#B and E are in the second position, so they correspond.
~B. D#D corresponds to A, not B.
~C. F#F corresponds to C, not B.
~D. A#A is in the first triangle and corresponds to D.
}

::GEO_U03_L03_Q02::
Question ID: GEO_U03_L03_Q02
MLA Standard: Readiness Support Only
Question:
If triangle PQR ≅ triangle XYZ, which side corresponds to QR?
{
~A. XY#XY corresponds to PQ.
=B. YZ#QR uses the second and third vertices, so it corresponds to YZ.
~C. XZ#XZ corresponds to PR.
~D. PQ#PQ is in the first triangle, not the corresponding side in the second.
}

::GEO_U03_L03_Q03::
Question ID: GEO_U03_L03_Q03
MLA Standard: Readiness Support Only
Question:
If triangle LMN ≅ triangle RST, which angle corresponds to angle N?
{
~A. angle R#R corresponds to L.
~B. angle S#S corresponds to M.
=C. angle T#N and T are in the third position, so they correspond.
~D. angle L#L is a vertex in the first triangle.
}

::GEO_U03_L03_Q04::
Question ID: GEO_U03_L03_Q04
MLA Standard: Readiness Support Only
Question:
Which congruence statement correctly matches A to D, B to E, and C to F?
{
~A. triangle ABC ≅ triangle DFE#This matches B to F and C to E.
~B. triangle ABC ≅ triangle EFD#This matches A to E, not D.
~C. triangle ABC ≅ triangle FED#This reverses several corresponding vertices.
=D. triangle ABC ≅ triangle DEF#The vertices are listed in matching order: A-D, B-E, C-F.
}

::GEO_U03_L03_Q05::
Question ID: GEO_U03_L03_Q05
MLA Standard: Readiness Support Only
Question:
What information is needed before concluding two figures are congruent?
{
=A. Evidence that corresponding sizes and shapes match.#Congruence requires same size and shape supported by evidence.
~B. They are drawn near each other.#Location on the page does not prove congruence.
~C. They have the same color.#Color is not a geometric property for congruence.
~D. They look similar at a glance.#Appearance alone is not enough evidence.
}

::GEO_U03_L03_Q06::
Question ID: GEO_U03_L03_Q06
MLA Standard: Readiness Support Only
Question:
If triangle JKL ≅ triangle MNO, which side corresponds to JK?
{
~A. MO#MO connects first and third vertices, matching JL.
=B. MN#J-K matches M-N by order.
~C. NO#NO matches KL.
~D. KL#KL is a side in the first triangle.
}

::GEO_U03_L03_Q07::
Question ID: GEO_U03_L03_Q07
MLA Standard: Readiness Support Only
Question:
If triangle RST ≅ triangle UVW, which angle corresponds to angle S?
{
~A. angle U#U corresponds to R.
~B. angle W#W corresponds to T.
=C. angle V#S and V are both second in the statement.
~D. angle T#T corresponds to W, not S.
}

::GEO_U03_L03_Q08::
Question ID: GEO_U03_L03_Q08
MLA Standard: Readiness Support Only
Question:
Which side corresponds to AC if triangle ABC ≅ triangle FDE?
{
~A. FD#FD matches AB.
~B. DE#DE matches BC.
~C. AB#AB is in the first triangle.
=D. FE#A-C matches F-E because A corresponds to F and C corresponds to E.
}

::GEO_U03_L03_Q09::
Question ID: GEO_U03_L03_Q09
MLA Standard: Readiness Support Only
Question:
Why does the order of letters in a congruence statement matter?
{
=A. It identifies corresponding vertices and parts.#The order tells which vertices, sides, and angles match.
~B. It changes the size of the triangles.#Statement order does not change actual size.
~C. It determines the color of the diagram.#Color is not part of geometric correspondence.
~D. It replaces the need for evidence.#Evidence is still needed to establish congruence.
}

::GEO_U03_L03_Q10::
Question ID: GEO_U03_L03_Q10
MLA Standard: Readiness Support Only
Question:
If triangle ABC ≅ triangle DEF, which statement follows by corresponding parts?
{
~A. angle A ≅ angle E#E corresponds to B, not A.
=B. angle A ≅ angle D#A corresponds to D, so those angles are congruent.
~C. AB ≅ EF#AB corresponds to DE, not EF.
~D. BC ≅ DE#BC corresponds to EF, not DE.
}

::GEO_U03_L03_Q11::
Question ID: GEO_U03_L03_Q11
MLA Standard: Readiness Support Only
Question:
If two triangles are congruent, what must be true?
{
~A. Only their areas are equal.#Equal area alone does not guarantee congruence.
~B. Only their angles are equal.#Equal angles alone can indicate similarity, not necessarily congruence.
=C. Corresponding sides and angles are congruent.#Congruent triangles have all corresponding sides and angles congruent.
~D. They must be in the same position.#Congruent figures can be moved by rigid transformations.
}

::GEO_U03_L03_Q12::
Question ID: GEO_U03_L03_Q12
MLA Standard: Readiness Support Only
Question:
A diagram marks AB ≅ DE and BC ≅ EF. Which side pair would complete corresponding side evidence for triangle ABC and triangle DEF?
{
~A. AC ≅ EF#EF already corresponds to BC.
~B. AB ≅ DF#AB corresponds to DE.
~C. BC ≅ DE#BC corresponds to EF.
=D. AC ≅ DF#A-C corresponds to D-F by the vertex order.
}

::GEO_U03_L03_Q13::
Question ID: GEO_U03_L03_Q13
MLA Standard: Readiness Support Only
Question:
Which notation correctly states corresponding angles from triangle CAT ≅ triangle DOG?
{
=A. angle A ≅ angle O#A and O are both second in the congruence statement.
~B. angle C ≅ angle O#C corresponds to D.
~C. angle T ≅ angle D#T corresponds to G.
~D. angle A ≅ angle G#A corresponds to O, not G.
}

::GEO_U03_L03_Q14::
Question ID: GEO_U03_L03_Q14
MLA Standard: Readiness Support Only
Question:
What does CPCTC mean in a congruence context?
{
~A. Congruent parts create triangle classification.#This phrase does not state the CPCTC relationship.
=B. Corresponding parts of congruent triangles are congruent.#CPCTC describes matching sides and angles after triangle congruence is known.
~C. Corresponding points change triangle coordinates.#Coordinates are not the meaning of CPCTC.
~D. Congruent triangles are always equilateral.#Congruent triangles do not have to be equilateral.
}

::GEO_U03_L03_Q15::
Question ID: GEO_U03_L03_Q15
MLA Standard: Readiness Support Only
Question:
If triangle HIK ≅ triangle PQR, which side corresponds to IK?
{
~A. PQ#PQ corresponds to HI.
~B. PR#PR corresponds to HK.
=C. QR#I-K corresponds to Q-R by order.
~D. HI#HI is in the first triangle.
}

::GEO_U03_L03_Q16::
Question ID: GEO_U03_L03_Q16
MLA Standard: Readiness Support Only
Question:
Which statement relies only on appearance and should be avoided?
{
~A. The triangles are congruent because all corresponding sides are marked congruent.#Corresponding side markings are valid evidence.
~B. The triangles are congruent because a rigid motion maps one onto the other.#Rigid-motion mapping is valid congruence evidence.
~C. The triangles are congruent because matching sides and angles are given.#Given matching parts can support congruence.
=D. The triangles look the same, so they are congruent.#Appearance alone is not enough for congruence.
}

::GEO_U03_L03_Q17::
Question ID: GEO_U03_L03_Q17
MLA Standard: Readiness Support Only
Question:
If triangle ABC ≅ triangle QRS, which side corresponds to CA?
{
=A. SQ#C-A corresponds to S-Q by order.
~B. RQ#RQ corresponds to BA.
~C. RS#RS corresponds to BC.
~D. AB#AB is in the first triangle.
}

::GEO_U03_L03_Q18::
Question ID: GEO_U03_L03_Q18
MLA Standard: Readiness Support Only
Question:
Which vertex correspondence is shown by triangle WXY ≅ triangle JKL?
{
~A. W <-> K#W corresponds to J.
=B. X <-> K#X and K are both second.
~C. Y <-> J#Y corresponds to L.
~D. X <-> L#X corresponds to K, not L.
}

::GEO_U03_L03_Q19::
Question ID: GEO_U03_L03_Q19
MLA Standard: Readiness Support Only
Question:
If two figures are congruent after a translation, what does the translation preserve?
{
~A. Only color#Color is not a geometric property.
~B. Only orientation#Translations preserve orientation but also preserve more than orientation.
=C. Distance and angle measure#Translations are rigid transformations, so they preserve distances and angles.
~D. Only area with no side lengths#Congruence requires side lengths and angle measures, not area alone.
}

::GEO_U03_L03_Q20::
Question ID: GEO_U03_L03_Q20
MLA Standard: Readiness Support Only
Question:
Which side pair corresponds if triangle BCA ≅ triangle EFD?
{
~A. BC ≅ FD#BC corresponds to EF.
~B. BA ≅ EF#BA corresponds to ED.
~C. CA ≅ EF#CA corresponds to FD, not EF.
=D. CA ≅ FD#C-A corresponds to F-D by order.
}

::GEO_U03_L03_Q21::
Question ID: GEO_U03_L03_Q21
MLA Standard: Readiness Support Only
Question:
What is the best first step when reading a congruence statement?
{
=A. Match vertices by their order in the statement.#Vertex order determines correspondence.
~B. Assume the drawings are to scale.#Drawings are not assumed to scale.
~C. Choose the longest side first.#Longest side is not always identified or relevant first.
~D. Ignore vertex order.#Ignoring order causes correspondence errors.
}

::GEO_U03_L03_Q22::
Question ID: GEO_U03_L03_Q22
MLA Standard: Readiness Support Only
Question:
If triangle MNP ≅ triangle ZYX, which angle corresponds to angle M?
{
~A. angle Y#Y corresponds to N.
=B. angle Z#M and Z are both first in the statement.
~C. angle X#X corresponds to P.
~D. angle P#P corresponds to X.
}

::GEO_U03_L03_Q23::
Question ID: GEO_U03_L03_Q23
MLA Standard: Readiness Support Only
Question:
Which side corresponds to NP in triangle MNP ≅ triangle ZYX?
{
~A. ZY#ZY corresponds to MN.
~B. ZX#ZX corresponds to MP.
=C. YX#N-P corresponds to Y-X.
~D. MN#MN is in the first triangle.
}

::GEO_U03_L03_Q24::
Question ID: GEO_U03_L03_Q24
MLA Standard: Readiness Support Only
Question:
A student says triangle ABC ≅ triangle DEF, so AC ≅ EF. What is the mistake?
{
~A. AC corresponds to DE, not EF.#DE matches AB.
~B. AC has no corresponding side.#Every side has a corresponding side in congruent triangles.
~C. EF is in the first triangle.#EF is in the second triangle.
=D. AC corresponds to DF, not EF.#A-C matches D-F by statement order.
}

::GEO_U03_L03_Q25::
Question ID: GEO_U03_L03_Q25
MLA Standard: Readiness Support Only
Question:
Which evidence can support congruence without relying on appearance?
{
=A. A rigid transformation maps one figure exactly onto the other.#Rigid-motion mapping is valid congruence evidence.
~B. The figures are close together.#Location does not prove congruence.
~C. The figures have similar names.#Names alone do not prove size and shape.
~D. The figures are both drawn in black.#Color is not geometric evidence.
}


